Suggested fixes
  1. No bot challenge blocks automated browsers [challenge-or-bot-wall, weight 10]
     Exempt well-behaved agents (honest UA, Web Bot Auth signatures) from challenges on public
     pages, or serve a machine-readable alternative.
  2. No overlay blocks the page at load [overlay-interference, weight 10]
     Avoid full-page overlays at load; if a consent dialog is required, make it a role="dialog"
     with a clearly named Accept/Reject button and let the rest of the page work beneath it.
  3. Visible labels are part of accessible names [label-in-name, weight 3]
     Ensure the accessible name (aria-label) contains the visible text of the control.
  4. Menus do not depend on hover [hover-only-menus, weight 3]
     Expose submenus through a <button aria-expanded> toggle, or render submenu links in the
     page (footer sitemap) so they are reachable without hover.
  5. Structured data (JSON-LD) describes the page [structured-data, weight 3]
     Add JSON-LD for Organization/WebSite on the home page and Product/Offer, FAQPage,
     ContactPoint or BreadcrumbList on inner pages.
  6. Clickable elements are real controls, not generic elements [fake-interactive-elements, weight 7]
     Use <button> or <a href> for actions and navigation. If a custom element must stay, add
     role="button", tabindex="0", a name and keyboard handlers.
  7. Navigation links are named and resolvable [navigation-links-usable, weight 7]
     Use real URLs in href for navigation items; name every nav link; if JavaScript handles
     navigation, keep a working href as fallback.
  8. Controls are native or complete ARIA widgets [native-controls, weight 7]
     Prefer native <select>, <input type="date|range|number">; when custom widgets are
     unavoidable, implement the full ARIA pattern (roles, states such as
     aria-valuenow/aria-expanded, keyboard support).

